  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nExtremely abnormal heat set records at 2 of our climate sites today \n(MSP and STC both with 90) and looks to stick around through at \nleast Sunday. The temperature at MSP while this AFD is being written \nis currently 75. The normal high for this time of year is 59... \nWinds are still also quite breezy out of the south with a few areas \nseeing gusts reach 25-30 MPH. These winds will lessen progressing \nthrough Sunday as the surface pressure gradient relaxes. Though, \ngusts of 20-25 MPH are possible over the eastern half of the CWA. \nHighs will climb well into the 80s for most of the area and all 3 of \nour climate sites (MSP, STC, and EAU) have a high chance of setting \ndaily records. Have blended in some NBM 90th percentile into the NBM \nto bump up highs today, resulting in MSP forecast to reach 87 and \nSTC and EAU reaching 85. Clouds will also be increasing today as a \ncold front slowly nears from the west. This front should reach \nwestern MN tonight, causing lows to dip into the 50s. Farther east, \nlows will remain stuffy in the mid to upper 60s. Large uncertainty \nexists for Monday's temperatures as it remains a question of how far \neast the cold front progresses. Slower frontal movement will mean \nthat our highs remain warm (at least upper 70s) for the southern and \neastern halves of the CWA. But, a faster front will mean the quicker \narrival of cooler air, keeping highs more in the 60s to mid 70s. \nEither way, it looks like there will be a strong northwest to \nsoutheast temperature gradient across our region Monday. It's just a \nmatter of where it sets up.\n\nChances for rain will also increase Monday afternoon into the \nevening as synoptic lift increases over the frontal boundary. The \nnorthern halves of MN and WI currently are the most likely to see \nwidespread rainfall. But, longer range CAMs do hint at more \nscattered to isolated activity reaching down into central MN and \nwestern WI. Significant rainfall isn't expected (perhaps up to a \n0.25\") but a few thunderstorms are possible given the non-zero CAPE. \nThe front moves completely east of our region Monday night, cooling \nus down and drying us out into mid-week. Highs Tuesday and Wednesday \nare forecast in the upper 50s to mid 60s while lows will largely be \nin the 40s.\n\nForecast models show our next chance of rain being associated with a \ntrough that turns into a cutoff low over the Rockies during the \nTuesday-Wednesday time frame. This feature is forecast to slowly \nmove east into the Central Plains before eventually getting picked \nup by a trough from the northern jetstream. How quickly the cutoff \nlow advances east and where it gets absorbed by the northern \njetstream remains in question. As of now, the energy looks to pass \nthrough the Upper Midwest around Thursday, which would bring at \nleast a decent chance of rain to much of our CWA. However, NBM's \nPoPs have trended slightly less, likely owing to model spread \nregarding the cutoff low's speed and timing/placement of phasing \ninto the northern stream. We will continue to monitor these features \nas they could put a damper on the region. For Friday into next \nweekend, there is high confidence in dry weather while there is low \nconfidence in how temperatures evolve. If the cutoff low and \nnorthern stream trough phase similar to the GFS and GEM, amplified \ntroughing would develop over the Great Lakes. This would bring \nstrong northerly flow and CAA into the Upper Midwest Friday through \nSunday. We'll have to we see if our first real cold snap of this \nFall occurs at the end of this week or we remain milder.",
